  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 925 AM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe Flood Watch remains in effect until 6 AM Wednesday morning for \nthe entire region. Abundant moisture from Hurricane Polo's\nremnants will exacerbate flash flooding for the region today.\n\nThe 12Z KEPZ sounding shows PW at 1.55 inches, beating the daily \nrecord of 1.38 inches for September 29, thanks to this continued \ntropical moisture being drawn up by a Pacific trough moving \nthrough southern Nevada and western Arizona. An additional 1-2 \ninches of rain is expected to fall across much of southern New \nMexico and far west Texas through late tonight. Some localized \nareas in the Gila National Forest may receive up to 3 inches.\n\nPolo's circulation is expected to arrive in far west Texas around 6 \nPM this afternoon, which will bring the risk of up to 60 mph \ngusty winds and isolated severe thunderstorms to El Paso County, \nHudspeth County, and Otero County. These counties, as well as the \neastern reaches of Dona Ana and Sierra Counties, are under a Wind \nAdvisory from 3 PM this afternoon through midnight tonight.\n\nThe rain is expected to move out of the region in the overnight \nhours Wednesday morning as Polo's dying circulation and the Pacific \ntrough push some dry air into southern New Mexico. Tropical\nmoisture will be to our east, but there will still be a slight\nchance of a shower or thunderstorm on Wednesday. The flood threat\nshould be over.\n\nThe Pacific trough moves well to our east on Thursday as an\nupper-level high builds over southern California, which should\nkeep conditions dry from Thursday through Saturday. The trough \nbecomes positively tilted though, and both models have a weak \ncutoff low drifting back to eastern New Mexico on Saturday, so a \nfew showers may be possible in the eastern part of the region.\n\nFurther out, both the extended GFS and ECMWF have the upper-level\nhigh steering Rachel away from land, and is not expected to be a \nthreat to the region. At this time, both the GFS and ECMWF have \nsome weak southerly flow surrounding the high that could advect \nmoisture into southern New Mexico around Monday, but at this time,\nit remains to be seen how much moisture the region will get.",
